      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 3 of 4 of an NMi OIML certificate for water meters (R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 14, project 4009997). This fragment contains:\n\n1. Certificate metadata: number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 14, project number 4009997, page 8 of 13 (so page_total 13), member state Netherlands.\n\n2. Table 7: General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - AC mains powered. Meter sizes DN40 through DN1100 with accuracy class 1 & 2 or separate class 1/class 2 rows, with Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 flow rates in m³/h and ratio Q3/Q1.\n\n3. Page 9 continues the table (DN1200 through DN1800) — continuation of Table 7 presumably.\n\n4. A note about flow rates freely chosen conditions.\n\n5. Table 8: Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered — DN40 through DN150 (page 9), continuing on page 10 with DN200 through DN1650.\n\n6. Another note about flow rates freely chosen.\n\nSo this is a matrix table case (like R137 example) but even more complex: attributes vary by meter size AND power supply configuration (AC mains vs battery) AND accuracy class.\n\nThe schema has:\n- certificate: number, scheme (A), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id\n- characteristics: config_level[] — attribute × config-axis matrix\n\nThe configuration axis here is \"power supply\" (AC mains vs battery/renewable energy), and within each, per meter size, accuracy class variants.\n\nThe best approach:\n- matrix_tables[] with two entries: \"Full bore - AC mains powered\" and \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" with columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1; column units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n- characteristics.model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio per meter size (aggregating across accuracy classes... but some sizes have different values per class).\n\nHmm, the model family: models are meter sizes DN40...DN1800. But for some sizes there are two rows (class 1 and class 2) with different Q1/Q2 values and different ratios.\n\nOptions:\n- Model = meter size × power supply × accuracy class. That would be DN40-AC-class1, DN40-AC-class2, etc. That's a lot of models but precise.\n- Or model_level attributes keyed by meter size, with config_level for power supply.\n\nGiven the schema guidance, config_level is for configuration axes like \"static vs dynamic weighing\". Here the axis is power supply type. Accuracy class is another dimension.\n\nI think a reasonable approach:\n\n1. model_family: models = meter sizes (DN40 ... DN1800). That's a big list: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800. That's 29 meter sizes.\n\n2. matrix_tables: two tables with rows per (meter size, accuracy class).\n\n3. characteristics.config_level: attribute = q1_min etc., axis = \"power_supply\" with conditions \"AC mains\" and \"battery / renewable energy\", each value per model... but config_level values take {condition, value}. Hmm, the schema says config_level values: {condition, value}. That doesn't include model. So maybe combine condition as \"AC mains - DN40\" etc. That gets messy.\n\nAlternatively, treat model_id as meter size, and put power supply as config axis. For model_level, for sizes where class 1 and class 2 differ, I could either:\n- List both values with model like \"DN150 (class 1)\" — but model should match model_id.\n- Or make model_id include class when they differ.\n\nActually, maybe the cleanest: models = combinations. But 29 sizes × 2 power × 2 classes = too many.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and faithful. The instructions say matrix detection is MOST IMPORTANT; extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level (per R137 example). In the R137 example, they keyed by size and only listed class-independent attributes.\n\nHere, the flow rates vary by size, power config, and accuracy class. The primary axis in the tables is meter size. I'll do:\n\n- model_family.models: one per meter size (29 entries), with model_id \"DN40\", etc. Maybe include attributes like accuracy_class [\"1\",\"2\"]... Actually attributes{} could note accuracy classes covered. But better keep simple.\n\n- matrix_tables: two entries (AC mains, battery), full rows.\n\n- characteristics.model_level: for each of q1_min, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 — but values differ across configs. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use model_id that includes config: e.g., model_id \"DN40-AC\", \"DN40-BAT\"... The schema example for R137 used size only.\n\nMaybe better: define models with attributes: {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\",\"2\"]}. But the family with distinct rows...\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The certificate covers a family of instruments. The models are effectively combinations: meter size × power supply (× accuracy class where flow rates differ). Since this fragment is part 3 of 4 (other parts probably define the family name and type designations), I should focus on the fragment content.\n\nI'll take this approach:\n\n- certificate: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", revision handled in... hmm, revision_history? The header says \"revision 14\". certificate.number could be \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" with revision... schema doesn't have a revision field at certificate level, but revision_history could include... we don't know dates of revisions. I could put number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe add revision_history entry {revision: \"14\"} without date? Rule 8 says don't fabricate. revision_history requires date and changes; we don't have those. Hmm. Maybe I can include revision in certificate number string: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 14\"? The number is typically \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". I'll include the number without revision and possibly note revision in... Actually schema for certificate: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Scheme: \"A\" (from \"2013-A\"). page_total: 13. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". date_issued not present. oiml_issuer_id not present.\n\nI could add revision_history with a single entry {revision: \"14\", date: null, changes: null}? Rule 8: empty strings are not values — use null or omit. Hmm, but revision_history entries with null date... risky. I think it's fine to include the revision in the certificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": not in schema. I'll just put the number as given: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Maybe include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"14\"}] — schema says revision (string), date, changes. Including only revision key omits date/changes keys entirely. That seems acceptable-ish but I'm torn. Since this fragment is part 3 of 4, the extraction target is \"content present in THIS fragment only\". The revision info IS present. I'll include revision_history with revision \"14\" and omit date/changes? Actually rule says omit fields not present. So {\"revision\": \"14\"}. Hmm, but is that a \"revision history\"? It's just the current revision. I'll include it minimally — it conveys info.\n\n- matrix_tables: two entries as described. Note page 10 table for DN350 row has a typo: `<td>250</td>` (malformed `</td><td>` — \"…<td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>\" written as `...5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — actually looking: `<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — wait the markdown shows `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>`? Let me re-read: `<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — the raw text is `<td>5000</td><td>250</td>`? The original: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` — hmm actually in the source it says `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` written as `...<td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>`. Wait, source line: `<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — let me check exact: \"…<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>\" Hmm the given text: `<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — Actually in the prompt: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` — wait it literally shows `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` as `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>`. Let me look again at the prompt text:\n\n`<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>`\n\nHmm, actually the prompt shows: `...<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — I see in the raw: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — no wait, the prompt literally has: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` but earlier I noticed `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` with a stray `</td>` — the raw shows: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>`? Let me scan: \"<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>\" — In the actual prompt it's: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — Hmm, I recall seeing `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` — the prompt shows:\n\n`<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>`\n\nWait, the actual text in the prompt: `<td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` — with `</td>` typo: \"…<td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>\" vs \"…<td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>\". Checking character by character from prompt: `<td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` — the prompt shows `</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td>` hmm I can't be sure. The line as given: `<tr><td>DN350</td><td>1 &amp; 2</td><td>16</td><td>25,6</td><td>4000</td><td>5000</td><td>250</td></tr>` — there appears to be a stray `</td>` making it `<td>5000</td><td>250</td>`. Either way, semantically: DN350, class 1&2, Q1=16, Q2=25.6, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio=250. The ratio cell is 250. Good — consistent with DN400 row (250). I'll record ratio 250.\n\nNow decimal conversion: comma decimals → dots. E.g., 0,08 → 0.08; 0,126 → 0.126; 0,2016 → 0.2016; 78,75 → 78.75; 0,5 → 0.5; 312,5 → 312.5; 2,52 → 2.52; 4,032 → 4.032; 6,4 → 6.4; 10,24 → 10.24; 158,7 → 158.7; 253,9 → 253.9; 406,2 → 406.2; 204,8 → 204.8; 0,8064 → 0.8064; 0,256 → 0.256; 0,252 → 0.252; 0,4032 → 0.4032; 12,8 → 12.8; 20,48 → 20.48; 25,6 → 25.6; 40,32 → 40.32; 39,375 → 39.375; 254 (no comma) stays 254; 406,3 → 406.3; 507,9 → 507.9; 100,8 → 100.8; 0,128 → 0.128; 0,512 → 0.512; 0,32 → 0.32; 0,64 → 0.64; 1,024 → 1.024; 1,6 → 1.6; 0,4 → 0.4; 0,16 → 0.16; 3,2 → 3.2; 5,12 → 5.12; 8,064 → 8.064; 0,08 → 0.08.\n\nNow building matrix_tables.\n\nTable A: \"Full bore - AC mains powered\" (Table 7, spans pages 8–9).\n\nColumns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min (Minimum Q1), q2 (Transitional Q2), q3 (Permanent Q3), q4 (Overload Q4), ratio_q3_q1. Units: q1–q4 in m³/h.\n\nRows (AC mains):\n- DN40, \"1 & 2\", 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, \"1 & 2\", 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\n- DN65, \"1 & 2\", 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\n- DN80, \"1 & 2\", 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, \"1 & 2\", 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n- DN125, \"1 & 2\", 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n- DN150, class 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN150, class 2: 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\n- DN200, class 1: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN200, class 2: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, class 1: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250, class 2: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, class 1: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN300, class 2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN350, \"1 & 2\", 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN400, \"1 & 2\", 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN450, \"1 & 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN500, \"1 & 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN600, \"1 & 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN700, class 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN700, class 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN750, class 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, class 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN800, class 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, class 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN900, class 1: 158.7, 253.9, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN900, class 2: 80, 128, 10000, 12500, 125\n- DN1000, class 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1000, class 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1050, class 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, class 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1100, class 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1100, class 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1200, class 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, class 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1350, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1350, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1400, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1500, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1600, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1650, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1800, \"1 & 2\", 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50\n\nTable B: \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" (Table 8, spans pages 9–10).\n\nRows (Battery):\n- DN40, class 1: 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125\n- DN40, class 2: 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250\n- DN50, class 1: 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125\n- DN50, class 2: 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250\n- DN65, \"1 & 2\": 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n- DN80, \"1 & 2\": 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n- DN100, \"1 & 2\": 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125, \"1 & 2\": 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN150, class 1: 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125\n- DN150, class 2: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200, class 1: 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125\n- DN200, class 2: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN250, class 1: 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125\n- DN250, class 2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300, class 1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n- DN300, class 2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350, \"1 & 2\": 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN400, \"1 & 2\": 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450, \"1 & 2\": 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500, \"1 & 2\": 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600, class 1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n- DN600, class 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN700, class 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN700, class 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, class 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN750, class 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, class 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN800, class 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN900, class 2 only: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN1000, class 2 only: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, class 2 only: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1100, class 2 only: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, class 2 only: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1350, class 2 only: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, class 2 only: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, class 2 only: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, class 2 only: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, class 2 only: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nNotes: two identical \"freely chosen\" bullet lists. Conditions:\n- Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013.\n- Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6.\n- Values for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25.\n- The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\n\nThese are description/footnote-like notes. I can put them in matrix_tables[].description or as footnotes. They're not numbered footnotes; I'll put them in each table's description (or one summary). I'll add description to both matrix tables with the conditions summarized.\n\nNow model_level characteristics. The R137 example populated model_level per size for class-independent attributes. Here, q3 and q4 per size are the same across accuracy classes (Q3 and Q4 depend only on